Image 1: the original product image. It is the sole authority for the garment design, color, material, proportions and every product detail.

Image 2: the floating-pants reference image. Use it only for the floating pose, composition, three-quarter perspective, spatial depth, fabric movement and lighting atmosphere.

TASK:

Transform the pants from Image 1 into a realistic, model-free floating product photograph using the dynamic perspective of Image 2.

This is an image-editing task, not a garment redesign.

The final image must still depict exactly the same pants as Image 1. Change only the floating pose, natural fabric folds, three-dimensional perspective and necessary spatial shadow.

PRODUCT DETAILS — STRICTLY LOCKED:

Preserve the exact original:

- warm ivory/off-white color;
- soft, matte, low-luster fabric;
- original loose straight-leg silhouette;
- original waistband height and width;
- elastic gathering pattern and stitch density;
- original rise, crotch depth and leg proportions;
- both side pockets, including their exact openings and stitching;
- dark charcoal piping around both trouser cuffs;
- original cuff width, construction, stitching and thickness;
- original labels, seams and all visible construction details.

Do not add, remove, simplify or redesign any part of the pants.

Do not inherit the pink color, satin sheen, garment construction, branding or decorative details from Image 2.

REQUIRED FLOATING POSE AND PERSPECTIVE:

Create a genuine asymmetrical three-quarter floating perspective, not a flat front view and not a symmetrical hanging pose.

The waistband should remain structurally stable and almost horizontal, but it must slope gently downward toward viewer-right by approximately 8–12 degrees.

Do not make the waistband perfectly horizontal, strongly arched, twisted or tilted in the opposite direction.

Rotate the upper hip and crotch plane consistently with the waistband. The waistband, pocket openings, center rise, crotch seam and upper-leg folds must all share the same three-dimensional perspective.

The viewer-left leg must sweep broadly downward and toward viewer-left in a smooth, extended curve. It should project slightly forward and outward, creating visible air and movement.

The viewer-left trouser cuff should be positioned higher than the viewer-right cuff and appear mildly foreshortened. Its opening may look slightly narrower or smaller because it is farther away.

The viewer-right leg should remain closer to the main vertical axis. It descends more vertically, with only a gentle outward curve.

The viewer-right trouser cuff should be lower, slightly closer to the viewer and subtly larger than the left cuff.

Create a clear but natural front-to-back relationship between the two legs. The legs must not look like two flat parallel strips placed on the same plane.

The crotch area must remain anatomically and structurally plausible. Preserve the original rise depth and avoid stretching, pinching, splitting or creating an unnatural empty hole.

The overall silhouette should form an asymmetrical flowing three-quarter composition:

- higher and more outward on viewer-left;
- lower and more vertical on viewer-right;
- dynamic but controlled;
- elegant, soft and commercially realistic.

Do not create a symmetric A-shape, an inverted V pose, parallel vertical legs or an exaggerated dancing pose.

FABRIC FOLDS AND DRAPE:

Generate long, soft gravity-informed folds beginning naturally from the elastic waistband, pocket area and crotch.

The folds must continue smoothly down each leg and reinforce the required curved direction.

The viewer-left leg may contain slightly stronger sweeping folds to show forward movement.

The viewer-right leg should contain quieter, longer vertical folds to maintain structural stability.

Keep the original fabric soft, lightweight and matte. Highlights must be broad and diffused, never sharp or metallic.

Avoid:

- random wrinkles;
- repeated artificial folds;
- sharp origami creases;
- inflated tube-shaped legs;
- melted or liquid fabric;
- overly smooth plastic surfaces;
- glossy satin highlights;
- thick, heavy curtain-like draping.

TROUSER-CUFF CONSTRUCTION — CRITICAL:

Preserve both original straight trouser-cuff constructions exactly.

The dark piping must remain at the same distance from the bottom edge and must follow the three-dimensional curvature of each cuff.

Each cuff opening should become a natural shallow ellipse because of perspective.

The viewer-left cuff opening should be slightly more foreshortened and positioned higher.

The viewer-right cuff opening should be positioned lower, face the viewer slightly more and show subtly greater depth.

Keep the original cuff width, seam position, piping thickness and fabric thickness.

Both trouser legs must remain the same original physical length. Their different apparent heights must come only from pose and perspective.

Do not turn the cuffs into:

- flared or bell-bottom openings;
- tapered ankles;
- rolled cuffs;
- gathered cuffs;
- thick rigid rings;
- oversized hollow tubes;
- detached fabric layers;
- uneven or mismatched cuff designs.

Do not allow the piping to disappear, double, break, float away from the seam or change color.

POCKETS AND WAISTBAND:

Retain both original side pockets.

The pocket openings must follow the rotated hip plane and must not remain artificially symmetrical after the pants are tilted.

Preserve the original pocket size, depth, stitching and placement.

The elastic waistband should show fine, soft, evenly distributed gathers. It must not become a drawstring waist, paper-bag waist, ribbed knit band or rigid belt.

Do not add drawstrings, buttons, zippers, belt loops or decorative elements.

LIGHTING — CRITICAL:

Use clean high-key commercial studio lighting.

Create one very large diffused key light from upper-left and slightly in front of the garment.

Add a broad, soft frontal fill from viewer-right to preserve the ivory color and reveal construction details inside the shadows.

Lighting must wrap softly around the fabric and reveal volume through gentle tonal transitions.

Use:

- neutral white balance;
- clean ivory highlights;
- soft light-gray translucent shadows;
- broad low-contrast highlights;
- subtle depth inside the folds;
- controlled edge separation from the background.

The fabric must remain matte and soft, with restrained highlights.

Avoid:

- flat gray lighting;
- dark muddy shadows;
- green, blue, yellow or pink color casts;
- strong directional spotlighting;
- hard-edged shadows;
- metallic reflections;
- plastic shine;
- glossy satin highlights;
- heavy contrast;
- overexposed white areas with lost fabric detail;
- artificial HDR clarity.

BACKGROUND AND FLOATING SHADOW:

Use a clean pale gray-white seamless studio background with a very subtle central light gradient.

The pants must appear fully suspended in open space, with no visible contact with the ground.

Add a broad, diffused, low-opacity floating shadow behind and slightly below the garment.

The shadow should be soft, light gray, separated from the pants and consistent with the upper-left key light.

It should indicate spatial depth without appearing like a floor shadow.

Do not include:

- a visible floor line;
- ground contact;
- hard drop shadows;
- mirror reflections;
- dark halos;
- heavy vignette;
- background texture;
- hangers;
- clips;
- wires;
- mannequin parts;
- human body parts;
- support objects or props.

COMPOSITION AND OUTPUT:

Vertical 2:3 composition, preferably 2048 × 3072 pixels if supported.

Show the complete pants with both waistband and cuffs fully visible.

Leave comfortable clean margins around the garment.

The pants should occupy approximately 72–80% of the canvas height.

Position the visual center slightly above the canvas center to allow space for the lower floating shadow.

Maintain sharp, natural product detail without blocky grain, excessive sharpening, fake texture or compression artifacts.

Final result: a premium, photorealistic e-commerce floating product photograph of the exact pants from Image 1, using only the pose, perspective, spatial movement and lighting atmosphere from Image 2.
